{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:squared-distance-phase-one","Squared distance from the phase\none","<p><em> </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"bottom top\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></munder><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></munder><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#lem:squared-distance-bound-separated-pairs","Squared-distance bound for separated\npairs","<p><em> For a finite family <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>z</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>T</mi><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></math></span>, a real number <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>δ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, and any set of pairs <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>P</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>T</mi></math></span> each separated by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></munder><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:finite-divisor-sum","The finite divisor sum","<p><em> For\nan integer <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, the contribution\nof the divisor indices to <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></math></span> is </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span>, both <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> equal <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi></math></span>. Hence <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>.\nSumming over the divisors gives <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></math></span>, not the whole tail difference: the\nnondivisor terms must still be included.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:separation-larger-than-error-implies","Separation larger than the error\nimplies exclusion","<p><em> Suppose </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\" fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" symmetric=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\" fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" symmetric=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mtext>for every </mtext></mstyle><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:coprime-pair-counting-totient","Coprime-pair counting and the totient","<p><em> For every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">#</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>:</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Here <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> is allowed: the boundary pair <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> accounts for the value <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:two-lattice-sums","Two lattice sums","<p><em> For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, the two choices of boundary\ngive </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"bottom top\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mtable columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"0.1em\"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></munder><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mtable columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"0.1em\"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></munder><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>.</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The removed pair is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Partitioning all strictly positive\npairs by their greatest common divisor gives </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mtext> </mtext><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mtable columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"0.1em\"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></munder><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> This total is\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span> exactly when <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:classical-coprime-pair-lambert-identity","The classical coprime-pair Lambert\nidentity","<p><em> For every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>coprime</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\"><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace></mstyle><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">(</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></math></span>, an\nelementary rational function of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span>,\nhence rational at every rational <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span>\nincluding <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. This is the\nclassical visible-point identity, and its Lean proof is a formalisation\nof it rather than a new result: writing each pair <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> of positive integers uniquely as\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>g</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> converts the quadrant sum\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></math></span>\ninto the displayed sum over visible points. With the plain weight <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup></math></span>, the same strictly positive index\nset instead sums to <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></math></span>. Thus at\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> the Lambert-weighted sum is\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>, whereas the plain-weight sum is\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, not <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span>. Adding the boundary pair <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> recovers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span> in the plain-weight sum.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:nondivisors-short-lcm-window","Nondivisors in a short LCM window","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. If <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>∤</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>t</mi></math></span> for a prime <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span> and an integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Every integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></math></span> divides <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span>. To see the first claim, some\nprime-power divisor <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup></math></span> of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span> exceeds <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span>; otherwise every prime-power divisor\nwould divide <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span>. Since <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup></math></span>, its remaining cofactor\nis <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span> and every prime\ndivisor of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span> also divides <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>j</mi></math></span>, then, for every integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nIndeed, the second factor’s argument is coprime to <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span>, so totient multiplicativity applies.\nThe hypothesis is essential to this factorisation: at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> the left side is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> but the displayed product\nwould be <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:unbounded-prime-support-mersenne-factors","Unbounded prime support in Mersenne\nfactors","<p><em> Every prime divisor <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span> of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, for prime <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi></math></span>, satisfies <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> (the order of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span> mod <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span> is exactly <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi></math></span>, by Fermat/Lagrange in <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>×</mo></msup></math></span>); consequently the\nprime divisors appearing in the layers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> are unbounded, unconditionally,\nwith no cyclotomic resultant hypothesis left open.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:prime-satisfying-stated-cyclotomic-conditions","A prime satisfying the stated\ncyclotomic conditions","<p><em> For every period <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> and threshold <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>, there exist a prime <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi></math></span> and a prime factor <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span> of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></math></span> (the binary cyclotomic\nlayer) with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span> coprime to <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mi>q</mi></math></span>, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, and <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>. The\ncharacteristic-prime exceptional case in the cyclotomic order\ndecomposition is eliminated directly, by choosing <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup></math></span> (rules out <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></math></span>) and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>h</mi></math></span> (rules out <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>h</mi></math></span>).</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:sufficient-order-hypothesis-unbounded-prime","A sufficient order hypothesis for\nunbounded prime support","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, and fix\nintegers <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. Suppose that for every pair of\nprimes <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> there is an integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi></math></span> such that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>m</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∣</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The\ndivisibility forces <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>.\nFor such <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span>, existence of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi></math></span> in the displayed range is equivalent to\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>ord</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></math></span>. It\nalso gives <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></math></span>. Hence every\nfixed finite set of primes is disjoint from the prime divisors of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for all sufficiently large prime\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi></math></span>. If, in addition, for all\nsufficiently large prime <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi></math></span> one has\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, then for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span> there are primes <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>B</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Indeed, the divisibility gives <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></math></span>. For a finite set of primes, take <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi></math></span> larger than all their <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi></math></span>-th powers; for the last assertion, take\na prime divisor of the nontrivial value <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> after this threshold. The stated\ncoprimality is included in the formal source’s layer hypothesis,\nalthough this last extraction argument uses only <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, the first theorem above\nsupplies the hypothesis with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>:\nhere <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>ord</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>,\nwhereas <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>ord</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></math></span>.\nFor <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></math></span> and a general\nfixed <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, the source uses the\n<em>eventual</em> version: the divisibility is required only for prime\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi></math></span> above a fixed threshold. The same\nproof then applies after that threshold. It is not valid to replace this\nby an all-prime assertion: <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>, but <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>6</mn><mo>∤</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. For the binary cyclotomic\nfamily, choosing <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>\nremoves these exceptional indices. Other families require their own\nproof of the divisibility and nontriviality assumptions.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:uniform-positive-gap-rank-one","A uniform positive gap for rank-one\nquotients","<p><em> For integers <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>\nand <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, write </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi></mrow></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> For every\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>480</mn></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nHere <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>, the proof uses <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1429</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1512</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3584</mn></math></span> when <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span>. In particular, these inequalities\nmake the denominator positive.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:bound-preserved-positive-normalised-averaging","The bound is preserved by positive\nnormalised averaging","<p><em> For a nonempty finite family of admissible\nquotients with positive weights summing to <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>, the weighted average still exceeds\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></span> by more than <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>480</mn></math></span>. If an admissible quotient is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></math></span> in lowest terms, with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>q</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>480</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:concatenation-specified-period-multiples","Concatenation and specified period\nmultiples","<p><em> For nonnegative integers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></math></span>, define the integer block sum </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></munderover><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nSplitting a block after its first <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></span>\nterms gives </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> In\nparticular, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></math></span>. The\ncorresponding cyclotomic identity is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>: the\norder-four factor at height <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi></math></span> is\nthe order-two factor at height <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>The order-three factor is different: <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> comes from\ntripling the period. It is not generally part of the doubling sequence.\nFor example, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></math></span> divides\nnone of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, because the\norder of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span> modulo <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>7</mn></math></span> is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>3</mn></math></span>, which does not divide <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup></math></span>. Thus the block identities relate\nspecified period multiples; they do not put all the order-two,\norder-three and order-four factors into one doubling chain.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:hgap-real","A real-part bound gives a certificate","<p><em> For all <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>X</mi></math></span> and the size condition <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup></math></span>, if </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>≤</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>h</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>N</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>L</mi></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:hgap-subset","The same implication for a nonempty\nsubset","<p><em> For any nonempty finite <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>T</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and the same size\ncondition, if </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></munder><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>≤</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> then\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>h</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>N</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>L</mi></math></span>. This generalises\nTheorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#thm:hgap-real\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#thm:hgap-real\">9.4</a>: <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>T</mi></math></span> can be <em>any</em> explicitly chosen\nnonempty finite subset of the dyadic block, not the whole block, and the\nproof (an averaging pigeonhole) never uses that <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>T</mi></math></span> has positive density or comes from a\npartition.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.json?v=ba7ef333d530"}
